{"timestamp":"2026-09-13T00:40:04.656Z","ordinal":5168,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a09736-d5c6-7cc3-a099-0521c0b02bd2","turn_id":"01a0982b-48a6-7a00-b4fb-0f363c602d5d","item":{"type":"Reasoning","id":"rs_0f3535a43349ee3d016aa5f0e274f087d29c013391ff084d82","summary_text":["**Fixing resume issues**\n\nI need to ensure the current resume reflects CPU usage instead of CUDA. I’ll start by updating the status, which incorrectly shows H100 when it should reflect CPU. The restart command needs adjustment to CPU OMP. I’ve noticed some instruction discrepancies, especially regarding time boundaries, which must align better. \n\nI also want to verify documentation updates and run various tests after the documentation fix to maintain functionality. Finally, I’ll update the DEMO.md to tell a new 2-minute story and clean up existing edits.","**Addressing documentation changes**\n\nI need to carefully preserve the CHECKPOINT and README due to their changes. Regarding `comparison.md`, I think it should revert only that file to its generated state since it's been altered with a narrative that I believe makes the byte-identical claim false. The user mentioned earlier that documentation wasn’t crucial, so I’m weighing that against the need for accuracy. \n\nI plan to remove the manual addition to maintain correctness without overwriting others' contributions. It’s tricky, but I want to ensure we stay fair to everyone involved!","**Updating documentation process**\n\nI might consider updating the generator to include a specific paragraph so that reruns match, but that could affect the comparison JSON. I need to locate the comparison generator first. Adding a narrative to it could be a test, but there’s some risk involved. Maybe it’s better to leave things as they are and note that the changes haven't been staged.\n\nSince the user is looking for a coherent story, I think it’s okay to patch the DEMO script without completely overwriting existing content. Let's go through the steps for the DEMO, while ensuring clarity and coherence!"],"raw_content":[]},"started_at_ms":1789260002517,"completed_at_ms":1789260004656}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-13T00:40:04.658Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"id":"rs_0f3535a43349ee3d016aa5f0e274f087d29c013391ff084d82","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0982b-48a6-7a00-b4fb-0f363c602d5d"},"summary":[{"text":"**Fixing resume issues**\n\nI need to ensure the current resume reflects CPU usage instead of CUDA. I’ll start by updating the status, which incorrectly shows H100 when it should reflect CPU. The restart command needs adjustment to CPU OMP. I’ve noticed some instruction discrepancies, especially regarding time boundaries, which must align better. \n\nI also want to verify documentation updates and run various tests after the documentation fix to maintain functionality. Finally, I’ll update the DEMO.md to tell a new 2-minute story and clean up existing edits.","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Addressing documentation changes**\n\nI need to carefully preserve the CHECKPOINT and README due to their changes. Regarding `comparison.md`, I think it should revert only that file to its generated state since it's been altered with a narrative that I believe makes the byte-identical claim false. The user mentioned earlier that documentation wasn’t crucial, so I’m weighing that against the need for accuracy. \n\nI plan to remove the manual addition to maintain correctness without overwriting others' contributions. It’s tricky, but I want to ensure we stay fair to everyone involved!","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Updating documentation process**\n\nI might consider updating the generator to include a specific paragraph so that reruns match, but that could affect the comparison JSON. I need to locate the comparison generator first. Adding a narrative to it could be a test, but there’s some risk involved. Maybe it’s better to leave things as they are and note that the changes haven't been staged.\n\nSince the user is looking for a coherent story, I think it’s okay to patch the DEMO script without completely overwriting existing content. Let's go through the steps for the DEMO, while ensuring clarity and coherence!","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning"}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-13T00:40:18.287Z","ordinal":5170,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_0f3535a43349ee3d016aa5f0e49b8c87d289aa75fd6fcf48b5","status":"completed","call_id":"call_YGGYTcX9JzqFUgveGECK1hxA","name":"exec","input":"const patch = \"*** Begin Patch\\n*** Update File: /Users/sametdegirmenci/Downloads/ysamet/docs/submission/DEMO.md\\n@@\\n | Time | Action | Suggested narration |\\n |---|---|---|\\n-| 0:00–0:15 | Show seven synchronized torque traces | “After a contact incident, an engineer needs a defensible account of what changed. The manual way is scrolling seven synchronized 1 kHz traces and assembling notes by hand. Trace turns a recorded interval into measurements, a model interpretation, and evidence they can share.” |\\n-| 0:15–0:30 | Open Event 01 marker details | “This mark comes from the dataset publisher. It is an annotation, not a verified cause or model detection.” |\\n-| 0:30–0:55 | Read the actual automatic analysis | “These torque ranges are calculated from 1,024 raw samples per joint. OpenTSLM separately predicts event type, affected joints, and timing. The two sources remain visible.” |\\n-| 0:55–1:10 | Click the input evidence link | “The answer links back to its exact input interval. Joint 2 has the largest measured range here; the model names J4 as its strongest disturbance. These are different quantities, and neither identifies the physical impact location.” |\\n-| 1:10–1:20 | Click Export investigation | “The report preserves the recording, interval, annotation, measurements, generated output, checkpoint and input receipt for a teammate.” |\\n-| 1:20–1:40 | Show the audited comparison | “We compared identical windows from 67 held-out recording groups. Simple signal features were strongest on classification — expected, since the labels derive from signal formulas. OpenTSLM produced a usable summary on 77.93% of windows; on windows it answered, its semantics accuracy was 98.5%. Its measured weakness is failing to emit a valid ‘no event’ answer on free motion, and we report that rather than hiding it.” |\\n-| 1:40–1:50 | Show TimeNet receipt | “Both original dataset parts passed build, load and raw-array validation. Code, configuration, predictions and scoring are reproducible.” |\\n-| 1:50–2:00 | Close on the product outcome and limitation | “The product hypothesis is faster correct investigation reports. The next validation is a timed engineer study. Today we demonstrate retrospective triage, not autonomous prevention or a verified physical diagnosis.” |\\n+| 0:00–0:15 | Start on the selected publisher event and click **Analyze interval** | “The robot stopped at 6.187 seconds. A controls engineer receives a timestamp and seven synchronized traces, then has to prepare a handoff for the controls lead or vendor.” |\\n+| 0:15–0:35 | Replay the 1.024-second interval at 0.5× while inference runs | “Trace keeps the publisher annotation, exact 7×1,024 model input and replay context together. The marker starts the investigation; it is not a verified cause or model detection.” |\\n+| 0:35–0:55 | Read the prediction and click **Show predicted moment in 3D** | “OpenTSLM predicts accidental contact, J4 and a relative onset. The robot jumps to that generated time cue. This is recorded articulation, not a reconstructed collision.” |\\n+| 0:55–1:15 | Point to **Review note**, then click **Inspect exact seven-channel input** | “The model highlights J4, while J2 has the largest measured torque range. Trace exposes that disagreement and links back to the exact signals instead of hiding it in prose.” |\\n+| 1:15–1:30 | Click **Compare with a reference window** | “Against an earlier raw window, J2's measured range increases by 2.963 newton-metres. We ask the engineer to confirm motion phase and payload before attributing a cause.” |\\n+| 1:30–1:42 | Click **Export incident handoff** | “The Markdown report carries the recording, interval, annotation, measurements, model revision, input receipt, review note and limitations to the next engineer.” |\\n+| 1:42–1:55 | Open **Evaluation** | “On 512 windows from 67 held-out recording groups, simple features win fixed classification. OpenTSLM adds the readable evidence-linked investigation object, but only 77.93 percent of generations are usable. We show that weakness.” |\\n+| 1:55–2:00 | Return to the incident | “Trace turns one recorded stop into a reviewable handoff. The business hypothesis is less report assembly and back-and-forth; the prepared pilot still has to measure it.” |\\n@@\\n-**Why use a TSLM when the feature baseline wins?** The experiment establishes that direct feature methods are very competitive on this controlled dataset, whose labels are themselves derived from signal formulas. The decomposed confusion matrices show the trained OpenTSLM checkpoint matches the baseline's accuracy on the windows it answers (98.5% on 399 of 512); its measured gap is failing to produce a valid structured \\\"no event\\\" summary on 46.5% of free-motion windows. That is an output-reliability problem, not evidence the model misreads contact events — and it is the checkpoint's documented next improvement, to be selected on validation. The temporal-language interface additionally provides readable interpretations and free-form questions that a feature classifier cannot. We do not claim the TSLM is necessary for calculating peaks or ranges.\\n+**Why use a TSLM when the feature baseline wins?** Use the feature baseline when the task is only a fixed label. Trace tests a different benefit: one model output that carries event type, relative timing and joint attribution into a readable, evidence-linked investigation handoff. We have not yet measured whether that handoff benefit justifies the lower reliability. We do not claim the TSLM is necessary for calculating peaks, ranges or the fixed benchmark labels.\\n*** End Patch\";\ntext(await tools.apply_patch(patch));\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0982b-48a6-7a00-b4fb-0f363c602d5d","create_time":1789259992.413854}}}
