and limitations to the next engineer.” |\n+| 1:42–1:55 | Open **Evaluation** | “On 512 windows from 67 held-out recording groups, simple features win fixed classification. OpenTSLM adds the readable evidence-linked investigation object, but only 77.93 percent of generations are usable. We show that weakness.” |\n+| 1:55–2:00 | Return to the incident | “Trace turns one recorded stop into a reviewable handoff. The business hypothesis is less report assembly and back-and-forth; the prepared pilot still has to measure it.” |\n \n@@ -27,3 +27,3 @@\n \n-**Why use a TSLM when the feature baseline wins?** The experiment establishes that direct feature methods are very competitive on this controlled dataset, whose labels are themselves derived from signal formulas. The decomposed confusion matrices show the trained OpenTSLM checkpoint matches the baseline's accuracy on the windows it answers (98.5% on 399 of 512); its measured gap is failing to produce a valid structured \"no event\" summary on 46.5% of free-motion windows. That is an output-reliability problem, not evidence the model misreads contact events — and it is the checkpoint's documented next improvement, to be selected on validation. The temporal-language interface additionally provides readable interpretations and free-form questions that a feature classifier cannot. We do not claim the TSLM is necessary for calculating peaks or ranges.\n+**Why use a TSLM when the feature baseline wins?** Use the feature baseline when the task is only a fixed label. Trace tests a different benefit: one model output that carries event type, relative timing and joint attribution into a readable, evidence-linked investigation handoff. We have not yet measured whether that handoff benefit justifies the lower reliability. We do not claim the TSLM is necessary for calculating peaks, ranges or the fixed benchmark labels.\n \n","move_path":null}},"status":"completed","stdout":"Success. Updated the following files:\nM /Users/sametdegirmen