{"timestamp":"2026-09-13T00:46:46.412Z","ordinal":5450,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_0f3535a43349ee3d016aa5f275891087d2b86ba98fc962205e","status":"completed","call_id":"call_U9mFZWq7NzEXbWPml6oVNUng","name":"exec","input":"const r = await tools.exec_command({\n  cmd: \"sed -n '1,240p' docs/submission/DEMO.md && sed -n '1,220p' docs/submission/VERIFICATION.md && sed -n '1,310p' inference/README.md\",\n  workdir: \"/Users/sametdegirmenci/Downloads/ysamet\",\n  yield_time_ms: 10000,\n  max_output_tokens: 25000\n});\ntext(r);\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0982b-48a6-7a00-b4fb-0f363c602d5d","create_time":1789260403.635792}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-13T00:46:46.430Z","ordinal":5451,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a09736-d5c6-7cc3-a099-0521c0b02bd2","turn_id":"01a0982b-48a6-7a00-b4fb-0f363c602d5d","session_id":"01a09736-d5c6-7cc3-a099-0521c0b02bd2","root_turn_id":"01a0982b-48a6-7a00-b4fb-0f363c602d5d","response_id":"resp_0f3535a43349ee3d016aa5f273845087d291dcc712e106f60e","usage":{"input_tokens":126877,"cached_input_tokens":126600,"cache_write_input_tokens":274,"output_tokens":161,"reasoning_output_tokens":50,"total_tokens":127038},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":15515005,"cached_input_tokens":15191451,"cache_write_input_tokens":308999,"output_tokens":46039,"reasoning_output_tokens":12210,"total_tokens":15561044},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":97625334,"cached_input_tokens":94263319,"cache_write_input_tokens":3327188,"output_tokens":345156,"reasoning_output_tokens":89102,"total_tokens":97970490}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-13T00:46:46.490Z","ordinal":5452,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a09736-d5c6-7cc3-a099-0521c0b02bd2","turn_id":"01a0982b-48a6-7a00-b4fb-0f363c602d5d","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-53fa2acd-732c-4faf-8ef7-d55d72c19430","process_id":"54666","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '1,240p' docs/submission/DEMO.md && sed -n '1,220p' docs/submission/VERIFICATION.md && sed -n '1,310p' inference/README.md"],"cwd":"file:///Users/sametdegirmenci/Downloads/ysamet","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,240p' docs/submission/DEMO.md","name":"DEMO.md","path":"docs/submission/DEMO.md"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,220p' docs/submission/VERIFICATION.md","name":"VERIFICATION.md","path":"docs/submission/VERIFICATION.md"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,310p' inference/README.md","name":"README.md","path":"inference/README.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"# Two-minute demo\n\n## Preparation\n\nFollow `inference/README.md` to restore the private tunnel; do not restart training or change the served checkpoint. Start the frontend with `VITE_API_BASE_URL=/api`. Run `python3 -m inference.smoke` and check the expected checkpoint digest. Open the initial `[5.787,6.811)` second selection with replay at 8 seconds. This recording is a development example, not the held-out accuracy demonstration.\n\nHave the audited comparison and the exported JSON report open beside the app. If the live service is unavailable, explicitly present the committed smoke result as a previously recorded run; never present it as fresh inference.\n\nTiming, measured 13 September 2026 on the current CPU-only deployment (two smoke runs through the SSH tunnel): one full analyze-interval round trip takes about **24 seconds**. For a live run, trigger **Analyze interval** while narrating the publisher-marker segment so the answer is ready by the analysis beat, or pre-run one analysis before starting the clock and run the second one live. Only one query is served at a time; do not queue clicks.\n\n## Script and actions\n\n| Time | Action | Suggested narration |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0:00–0:15 | Start on the selected publisher event and click **Analyze interval** | “An incident was annotated at 6.187 seconds. A controls engineer receives that timestamp and seven synchronized traces, then has to prepare a handoff for the controls lead or vendor.” |\n| 0:15–0:35 | Replay the 1.024-second interval at 0.5× while inference runs | “Trace keeps the publisher annotation, exact 7×1,024 model input and replay context together. The marker starts the investigation; it is not a verified cause or model detection.” |\n| 0:35–0:55 | Read the prediction and click **Show predicted moment in 3D** | “OpenTSLM predicts accidental contact, J4 and a relative onset. The robot jumps to that generated time cue. This is recorded articulation, not a reconstructed collision.” |\n| 0:55–1:15 | Point to **Cross-check**, then click **Inspect exact seven-channel input** | “The model predicts J4 as strongest, while J2 has the largest raw torque range. Those are different rankings. Trace links both back to the exact signals instead of hiding the distinction in prose.” |\n| 1:15–1:30 | Click **Compare with a reference window** | “Against an earlier raw window, J2's measured range increases by 2.963 newton-metres. We ask the engineer to confirm motion phase and payload before attributing a cause.” |\n| 1:30–1:42 | Click **Export incident handoff** | “The Markdown report carries the recording, interval, annotation, measurements, model revision, input receipt, review note and limitations to the next engineer.” |\n| 1:42–1:55 | Open **Evaluation** | “On 512 windows from 67 held-out recording groups, simple features win fixed classification. OpenTSLM adds the readable evidence-linked investigation object, but only 77.93 percent of generations are usable. We show that weakness.” |\n| 1:55–2:00 | Return to the incident | “Trace turns one recorded stop into a reviewable handoff. The business hypothesis is less report assembly and back-and-forth; the prepared pilot still has to measure it.” |\n\nDo not memorize a generated answer as a guaranteed future result. Read the actual response. The model and measured quantities can disagree; that is a reason to inspect evidence. The exact archived live response is in `demo/smoke-result.json`.\n\n## Jury questions\n\n**Why use a TSLM when the feature baseline wins?** Use the feature baseline when the task is only a fixed label. Trace tests a different benefit: one model output that carries event type, relative timing and joint attribution into a readable, evidence-linked investigation handoff. We have not yet measured whether that handoff benefit justifies the lower reliability. We do not claim the TSLM is necessary for calculating peaks, ranges or the fixed benchmark labels.\n\n**How is this faster than just looking at charts?** The manual workflow is scrolling seven synchronized 1 kHz traces and writing notes by hand; in Trace the same incident is marker → analyze → linked evidence → export in a few interactions, as shown in the demo. We deliberately state this as a workflow demonstration, not a measured time saving: the timed engineer study is prepared (`PILOT.md`) but has not been run.\n\n**What is agentic?** The existing workflow composes deterministic measurement tools with a specialized trained model. The separate dataset-scout PR handles bounded sourcing and review. Bounded documentation search is merged infrastructure, not a claimed live forensic RAG agent.\n\n**What business value is demonstrated?** A traceable investigation and export workflow is demonstrated. Reduced debugging time is a hypothesis with a defined pilot; no customer ROI or downtime reduction has been measured.\n\n**Can it find an unknown event anywhere in a run?** The demo opens publisher-marked intervals. General unannotated incident localization and multi-recording raw-window inference are not demonstrated by the bundled fixture.\n# Verification, 13 September 2026\n\nBase: main `db68e6c`; final hardening is on `codex/final-submission-hardening`. Entire is enabled for the worktree; Codex hooks are installed and approval records are present.\n\n## Checks performed\n\n| Check | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Comparison replay, Python standard library | Checksums, identical IDs/targets, test-session membership and manifests passed; 512 windows / 67 recordings |\n| Independent comparison rerun | JSON byte-identical with fixed bootstrap seed; 512 windows / 67 recordings |\n| Training focused suite | 26 passed across comparison, metrics and signal-feature baseline tests |\n| Data ingestion | 60 passed in the component virtual environment, including TimeNet coverage |\n| Inference, `python3 -m unittest discover -s inference -p 'test_*.py' -v` | 28 passed with temporary localhost server permission |\n| Frontend, `npm test` | 81 passed, including structured prediction isolation, model/measurement disagreement, investigation provenance and replay contracts |\n| Frontend, `npm run build` | Passed; existing optional 3D chunk remains over Vite's 500 kB advisory threshold |\n| Ruff, new comparison module/tests | Passed |\n| Clean environment dependency compatibility | `pip check` passed |\n| Real TimeNet round trip | Both dataset parts passed 17 checks each, including all torque/position values and annotation index/time conversion |\n| Private deployed model | Ready; expected canary-v4 SHA-256; real `inference.smoke` passed |\n| Browser, desktop | Structured prediction, J4/J2 review note, predicted 3D moment, seven-channel evidence, reference comparison and Markdown export verified through the live UI |\n| Actual downloaded investigation JSON | Verified 1,024 raw samples/channel, seven measurement rows, one in-window publisher marker, expected checkpoint and matching model receipt |\n\nThe complete optional training suite initially could not collect `test_checkpoints.py` without PyTorch. The focused suite above is the completed local check, not a claim that GPU training or that checkpoint test was rerun. The live smoke confirms actual inference with the existing deployed checkpoint.\n\nThe clean environment also exposed a pre-existing macOS test-path comparison (`/var` versus resolved `/private/var`); the assertion now compares resolved paths. PyArrow printed restricted CPU-cache probes under the sandbox but completed build/load/validation successfully.\n\n## Integration boundary\n\nPR #19 is the only open pull request. It joins approved dataset sourcing to one-shot ingestion and remains outside the frozen two-minute demo until its broad service and UI changes receive a dedicated review. Training artifacts are scored from their saved predictions; training processes and model deployment were not modified. CNN results remain evaluation-only because no CNN checkpoint or inference endpoint is connected.\n\n## Remaining evidence gaps\n\n- No timed engineer study, customer testimonial or measured ROI.\n- No plain-Llama numerical/text baseline; Qwen is the available zero-shot comparator and has an explicit contact-field failure.\n- No newly untouched confirmatory test after the team inspected these results.\n- No full-corpus TimeNet rebuild in this verification; two full recordings were validated.\n- Checkpoint delivery to the challenge is still an external submission step. The backbone file identity is resolved by hash in `CHECKPOINT_HANDOFF.md`, but the live receipt still reports an unknown backbone revision.\n- No multi-recording raw-window demo, live control, verified root cause or physical contact localization.\n# Trace → OpenTSLM on Nebius\n\nStatus (13 September 2026): deployed privately on the team's Nebius H100 VM in\n`/home/samet/trace-inference`, using Python 3.12 and an isolated virtual environment.\nThe KUKA OpenTSLM-SP adapter is loaded and the SSH tunnel, Vite proxy and real\n1,024-sample inference path pass. The service reports the exact checkpoint digest;\nit never silently switches to a newer training artifact. The current process uses\nCPU because a teammate's training run owns the GPU; do not restart it on CUDA until\nthe training owner confirms that GPU memory is available.\n\nA training endpoint does not serve predictions automatically: this separate process\nloads an exported checkpoint once, then handles on-demand requests from Trace.\n\n### Resume this deployment\n\nThe VM is `samet@89.169.110.3`. After your Hugging Face account has access to the\nbase model, authenticate privately (do not paste tokens into chat or source code):\n\n```bash\nssh -t samet@89.169.110.3 '~/trace-inference/inference/.venv/bin/hf auth login'\n```\n\nThe process is in the `trace-inference` tmux session. After authentication, restart\nthat process only; the inference service does not automatically reload failed\nweights. From inside the VM:\n\n```bash\ntmux respawn-pane -k -t trace-inference -c /home/samet/trace-inference 'exec env TRACE_MODEL_CONFIG=inference/kuka-sp-canary-v4.config.json TRACE_DEVICE=cpu OMP_NUM_THREADS=4 MKL_NUM_THREADS=4 inference/.venv/bin/python -m inference.server >> inference/server.log 2>&1'\ncurl http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/health\n```\n\nWait for `ready: true` before running the smoke test. The local frontend now uses\n`VITE_API_BASE_URL=/api` in gitignored `.env.local`; reconnect the SSH tunnel below\nif the local session ends. This VM has no passwordless sudo; its environment was\ncreated with the official user-local `uv` installer without changing system Python:\n\n```bash\n~/.local/bin/uv venv --python /usr/bin/python3 ~/trace-inference/inference/.venv\n~/.local/bin/uv pip install --python ~/trace-inference/inference/.venv/bin/python -r ~/trace-inference/inference/requirements.txt\n```\n\nDo not recreate the existing environment just to restart the service.\n\n## 1. Find the machine and checkpoint\n\nIn the Nebius console, choose your team's project, then **Compute → Virtual\nmachines**. Open a running VM and inspect its GPU type/count, public IP and SSH\nconnection instructions. If the list is empty, check other projects/regions.\nAn allocated training job is not necessarily a persistent VM you can SSH into.\n\nAsk Atakan for:\n\n> Which architecture are you training (OpenTSLM-SP or Flamingo), which base LLM,\n> and where is the exported checkpoint? Please share the training config, including\n> normalization, channel order, sampling/window length, prompt template and LoRA\n> settings. Is there a GPU VM we can use for inference without competing with training?\n\nThe first runtime supports **OpenTSLM-SP** with strict encoder/projector loading\nand explicit LoRA settings. The initial config uses the official\n`OpenTSLM/llama-3.2-1b-tsqa-sp` checkpoint. It was trained on TSQA, **not KUKA**;\nseven robot channels and this prompt are out-of-domain. A plausible answer is\nnot a model-quality result. Keep local numerical calculations as the factual check.\n\nFor Flamingo or a modified training fork, share the config first: upstream uses a\ndifferent state format and permissive checkpoint loading. This service deliberately\nfails on an unsupported architecture instead of silently serving partial weights.\n\n## 2. Prepare one GPU VM\n\nIf no suitable VM exists, create a plain GPU VM in Nebius with an Ubuntu CUDA\nimage and your **public** SSH key. One GPU is the starting point; check the available\nGPU's price, memory and quota before creation. A GPU cluster/Kubernetes is unnecessary\nfor this single-process smoke test. Use persistent storage for model caches and\ncheckpoints, and enough free disk for PyTorch/CUDA dependencies and weights (100 GiB\nis a practical starting allocation, not a measured minimum).\n\nPrefer a separate GPU or run after training pauses; two processes can exhaust VRAM.\nFrom your Mac, test the connection using the username from the VM configuration:\n\n```bash\nssh YOUR_USER@YOUR_VM_IP\nnvidia-smi\npython3 --version\n```\n\nThese commands assume Python 3.12 on the VM. The supplied requirements pin the\nupstream source and core ML dependencies; actual CUDA execution still needs its\nfirst GPU smoke test. If necessary on Ubuntu:\n\n```bash\nsudo apt-get update\nsudo apt-get install -y python3-venv git tmux\n```\n\n## 3. Copy this isolated service\n\nFrom the repository root on your Mac (this also works before committing):\n\n```bash\nssh YOUR_USER@YOUR_VM_IP 'mkdir -p ~/trace-inference/inference ~/trace-inference/frontend/public/data'\nscp inference/*.py inference/requirements.txt inference/smoke.config.json YOUR_USER@YOUR_VM_IP:~/trace-inference/inference/\nscp frontend/public/data/kuka-demo.json YOUR_USER@YOUR_VM_IP:~/trace-inference/frontend/public/data/\n```\n\nOn the VM:\n\n```bash\ncd ~/trace-inference\npython3 -m venv inference/.venv\nsource inference/.venv/bin/activate\npython -m pip install --upgrade pip\npython -m pip install -r inference/requirements.txt\npython -m pip check\npython -m pip freeze > inference/requirements.resolved.txt\npython -c 'import torch; print(torch.__version__); print(torch.cuda.is_available())'\nhf auth login\n```\n\nThe Llama backbone may require your Hugging Face account to have accepted its\nlicense/access terms. Authenticate on the VM interactively; never put its token in\nthe frontend, a URL, a screenshot or a committed config. Use only checkpoints from\nthe official publisher or your team's trusted training process.\n\nStart a persistent terminal session, then the server:\n\n```bash\ntmux new -s trace-inference\ncd ~/trace-inference\nsource inference/.venv/bin/activate\nTRACE_DEVICE=cuda python -m inference.server 2>&1 | tee inference/server.log\n```\n\nTo expose every authorized source recording through the same replay contract, mount\nthe trusted extracted KUKA archive and pass its root explicitly:\n\n```bash\nTRACE_DEVICE=cuda python -m inference.server --raw-root /absolute/path/to/kuka/raw\n```\n\nThe root must contain `<archive>/<recording-id>/JK_MsrExtTrq.mat` and the matching\n`JK_moments.mat`. At startup the adapter validates the original 1 kHz time axis,\nseven-channel shape, finite values, publisher marker bounds and file hashes. The\nfrontend receives a 100 Hz overview, then requests an exact 1,024-sample raw window\nonly for the selected analysis interval. It never resamples raw model input.\n\nDetach with Ctrl+B, then D. The server binds to **127.0.0.1:8000** by default. Keep\nit private; this development bridge is not an authenticated public API. It exposes\nreadiness at `/api/health` and actual availability at `/api/models` while weights\nload. No successful placeholder response is substituted on failure.\n\n## 4. Connect Trace and run the real smoke test\n\nOn your Mac, keep this tunnel running:\n\n```bash\nssh -N -L 127.0.0.1:8000:127.0.0.1:8000 YOUR_USER@YOUR_VM_IP\n```\n\nFrom the repository root, in another terminal:\n\n```bash\ncurl http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/health\npython3 -m inference.smoke\n```\n\nThe smoke command fails unless it gets a completed answer, checkpoint identity,\nthe exact selected evidence window and a receipt for **1,024 samples per channel**.\nIt saves `inference/smoke-result.json` (gitignored). Server logs also record an input\nhash. This checks the application path and interval handling; it is not an independent\nGPU attestation or an accuracy benchmark.\n\nThen restart Vite with connected services enabled:\n\n```bash\ncd frontend\nVITE_API_BASE_URL=/api npm run dev -- --port 5174\n```\n\nThe existing `/api` development proxy targets the local SSH tunnel. Alternatively\nset `VITE_API_BASE_URL=/api` in `frontend/.env.local`, then restart Vite. For a\ndeployed frontend, configure a same-origin reverse proxy; Vite's dev proxy is not\nincluded in a production build.\n\nOpen Trace and keep the initial interval **[5.787, 6.811) seconds**. The header must\nshow **OpenTSLM connected**. Click **Analyze interval** and wait for the structured\nprediction, measurements, input receipt and handoff actions. Only OpenTSLM is exposed\nby this service; there is no live CNN selector or endpoint. The measurements-only\nmode remains available under **Ask a custom question**.\n\nWith `--raw-root`, the backend catalog also lists the validated full recordings.\nOpen one, choose any publisher marker, and run the resulting 1.024-second interval.\nThe UI loads that exact raw interval on demand and preserves its input receipt in\nthe exported investigation. Publisher markers remain annotations and are never sent\nto the model.\n\n## 5. Switch to the team's checkpoint\n\nCopy `smoke.config.json` to a gitignored `inference/team.local.json`. Set:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"architecture\": \"sp\",\n  \"model_id\": \"team-kuka-sp\",\n  \"base_model\": \"meta-llama/Llama-3.2-1B\",\n  \"checkpoint_path\": \"/absolute/persistent/path/to/exported-checkpoint.pt\",\n  \"normalization\": \"zscore_sample\",\n  \"max_new_tokens\": 128\n}\n```\n\nThis example is **not** a guess at your team's training configuration. Confirm every\nfield and align `prepare_sample()` with its prompt and preprocessing before using\nthe trained model. Supported normalization options are `zscore_sample` (per channel,\nsample standard deviation; same numerical convention as upstream TSQA) and `none`.\nIf training used LoRA, add `lora` with the exact `lora_r`, `lora_alpha`,\n`lora_dropout` and `target_modules` passed to upstream `enable_lora()`.\n\n```bash\nTRACE_MODEL_CONFIG=inference/team.local.json TRACE_DEVICE=cuda python -m inference.server\n```\n\nStop the previous server before starting the replacement. Re-run the smoke test,\nthen evaluate held-out recordings against the CNN and numerical baseline.\n\n### Promote an evaluated training run\n\nNew training runs now write tensor-only `best_model.pt` and `last_model.pt` files\nthat the inference runtime can load with `weights_only=True`. After\n`post_training.py` reaches `state: complete`, stage the exact artifact and generate\nthe next service config:\n\n```bash\npython -m inference.release \\\n  --candidate /path/to/run/best_model.pt \\\n  --post-training-status /path/to/run/post_training_status.json \\\n  --active-config inference/kuka-sp-v2.config.json \\\n  --models-dir models \\\n  --output-config inference/kuka-sp-next.config.json\n```\n\nThe command refuses incomplete evaluations, unsafe checkpoint payloads and LoRA\nconfiguration mismatches. It stores an immutable digest-named checkpoint and a\nrelease manifest. Review the held-out metrics, point the tmux startup command at\nthe generated config, restart once, and run `python -m inference.smoke`. Keep the\nprevious config until the smoke test passes so rollback is one restart.\n\n## Boundaries and validation\n\n- Queries retrieve signals server-side by recording/window/channel IDs. Annotation\n  labels never enter model input; each request remains fixed if playback changes.\n- This bundled fixture contains raw 1 kHz data only in **[4, 9) seconds**. Inference\n  rejects other ranges and any window that is not exactly 1.024 seconds. It does not feed the reduced\n  100 Hz overview to the model. Full recording inference needs the ingestion/data\n  team to supply raw windows behind the same API.\n- No resampling: the selected channels are normalized individually and zero-padded\n  to a multiple of four for the upstream encoder. The initial interval needs no\n  padding. Ordering follows the submitted channel IDs.\n- One query at a time. Cancellation requests stop generation at the next decoding\n  step; the slot stays occupied until the model call returns. A GPU operation already\n  running cannot be forcibly interrupted by HTTP cancellation. The 120-second\n  stopping criterion also acts between decoding steps, not as a process watchdog.\n- Evidence links identify **input telemetry**, not verified causal explanations.\n  Checkpoint SHA256, configuration hash and resolved backbone revision accompany\n  model identity; an input hash/sample count is included in the completion receipt.\n- CPU-only tests use an explicitly injected test double and do not establish that\n  the checkpoint runs on CUDA. Run from the repository root:\n\n```bash\npython3 -m unittest discover -s inference -p 'test_*.py' -v\n```\n\nOfficial references: [Nebius VM quickstart](https://docs.nebius.com/compute/quickstart),\n[Nebius SSH connection](https://docs.nebius.com/compute/virtual-machines/connect),\n[OpenTSLM source](https://github.com/OpenTSLM/OpenTSLM/tree/2968f4b891baab4307f7e9d0043e87677b593a30),\n[TSQA checkpoint](https://huggingface.co/OpenTSLM/llama-3.2-1b-tsqa-sp).\n\n## Exact input contract and fixed UI examples\n\nEvery inference request now requires `joint_1` through `joint_7` in that order,\na half-open 1.024-second window, and exactly 1,024 contiguous raw samples at 1 kHz.\nBoth the HTTP bridge and model preparation reject mismatches; no resampling or\npadding is used to repair a selection. Display endpoints still accept arbitrary\nintervals and channel subsets. All model input must precede the replay cursor.\n\n`demo_cases.json` adds three fixed UI examples and records original source hashes,\nfixture hashes and selection policy. The two gzip files in `demo_data/` preserve\noriginal torque values (no decimal rounding), a 100 Hz overview, and `[0,8)` raw\ndetail. Build them with scipy/numpy installed:\n\n```sh\npython -m inference.build_demo_cases --raw-root /path/to/data/raw --splits /path/to/data/prepared/v1/splits.json\n```\n\nThe files are checked in; serving them adds no scientific Python dependency.\nDefault startup loads this catalogue; `--data` retains an isolated single-fixture\nserver for tests. `GET /api/demo-cases` provides fixed navigation selections and\nnotes. `GET /api/recordings/{id}/replay` returns the validated overview, raw detail,\nannotations and recording metadata without losing the raw excerpt. Existing\nrecording signals/events endpoints resolve each recording independently.\n\nVerify each example through the same frontend proxy:\n\n```sh\npython -m inference.smoke --base-url http://127.0.0.1:5174/api --case accidental --output /tmp/collision.json\npython -m inference.smoke --base-url http://127.0.0.1:5174/api --case intentional --output /tmp/contact.json\npython -m inference.smoke --base-url http://127.0.0.1:5174/api --case free --output /tmp/free.json\n```\n\nThese are connection checks, not evaluation. The intentional example's source\nrecording belongs to the test split; collision/free belong to training. None is\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"# Two-minute demo\n\n## Preparation\n\nFollow `inference/README.md` to restore the private tunnel; do not restart training or change the served checkpoint. Start the frontend with `VITE_API_BASE_URL=/api`. Run `python3 -m inference.smoke` and check the expected checkpoint digest. Open the initial `[5.787,6.811)` second selection with replay at 8 seconds. This recording is a development example, not the held-out accuracy demonstration.\n\nHave the audited comparison and the exported JSON report open beside the app. If the live service is unavailable, explicitly present the committed smoke result as a previously recorded run; never present it as fresh inference.\n\nTiming, measured 13 September 2026 on the current CPU-only deployment (two smoke runs through the SSH tunnel): one full analyze-interval round trip takes about **24 seconds**. For a live run, trigger **Analyze interval** while narrating the publisher-marker segment so the answer is ready by the analysis beat, or pre-run one analysis before starting the clock and run the second one live. Only one query is served at a time; do not queue clicks.\n\n## Script and actions\n\n| Time | Action | Suggested narration |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0:00–0:15 | Start on the selected publisher event and click **Analyze interval** | “An incident was annotated at 6.187 seconds. A controls engineer receives that timestamp and seven synchronized traces, then has to prepare a handoff for the controls lead or vendor.” |\n| 0:15–0:35 | Replay the 1.024-second interval at 0.5× while inference runs | “Trace keeps the publisher annotation, exact 7×1,024 model input and replay context together. The marker starts the investigation; it is not a verified cause or model detection.” |\n| 0:35–0:55 | Read the prediction and click **Show predicted moment in 3D** | “OpenTSLM predicts accidental contact, J4 and a relative onset. The robot jumps to that generated time cue. This is recorded articulation, not a reconstructed collision.” |\n| 0:55–1:15 | Point to **Cross-check**, then click **Inspect exact seven-channel input** | “The model predicts J4 as strongest, while J2 has the largest raw torque range. Those are different rankings. Trace links both back to the exact signals instead of hiding the distinction in prose.” |\n| 1:15–1:30 | Click **Compare with a reference window** | “Against an earlier raw window, J2's measured range increases by 2.963 newton-metres. We ask the engineer to confirm motion phase and payload before attributing a cause.” |\n| 1:30–1:42 | Click **Export incident handoff** | “The Markdown report carries the recording, interval, annotation, measurements, model revision, input receipt, review note and limitations to the next engineer.” |\n| 1:42–1:55 | Open **Evaluation** | “On 512 windows from 67 held-out recording groups, simple features win fixed classification. OpenTSLM adds the readable evidence-linked investigation object, but only 77.93 percent of generations are usable. We show that weakness.” |\n| 1:55–2:00 | Return to the incident | “Trace turns one recorded stop into a reviewable handoff. The business hypothesis is less report assembly and back-and-forth; the prepared pilot still has to measure it.” |\n\nDo not memorize a generated answer as a guaranteed future result. Read the actual response. The model and measured quantities can disagree; that is a reason to inspect evidence. The exact archived live response is in `demo/smoke-result.json`.\n\n## Jury questions\n\n**Why use a TSLM when the feature baseline wins?** Use the feature baseline when the task is only a fixed label. Trace tests a different benefit: one model output that carries event type, relative timing and joint attribution into a readable, evidence-linked investigation handoff. We have not yet measured whether that handoff benefit justifies the lower reliability. We do not claim the TSLM is necessary for calculating peaks, ranges or the fixed benchmark labels.\n\n**How is this faster than just looking at charts?** The manual workflow is scrolling seven synchronized 1 kHz traces and writing notes by hand; in Trace the same incident is marker → analyze → linked evidence → export in a few interactions, as shown in the demo. We deliberately state this as a workflow demonstration, not a measured time saving: the timed engineer study is prepared (`PILOT.md`) but has not been run.\n\n**What is agentic?** The existing workflow composes deterministic measurement tools with a specialized trained model. The separate dataset-scout PR handles bounded sourcing and review. Bounded documentation search is merged infrastructure, not a claimed live forensic RAG agent.\n\n**What business value is demonstrated?** A traceable investigation and export workflow is demonstrated. Reduced debugging time is a hypothesis with a defined pilot; no customer ROI or downtime reduction has been measured.\n\n**Can it find an unknown event anywhere in a run?** The demo opens publisher-marked intervals. General unannotated incident localization and multi-recording raw-window inference are not demonstrated by the bundled fixture.\n# Verification, 13 September 2026\n\nBase: main `db68e6c`; final hardening is on `codex/final-submission-hardening`. Entire is enabled for the worktree; Codex hooks are installed and approval records are present.\n\n## Checks performed\n\n| Check | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Comparison replay, Python standard library | Checksums, identical IDs/targets, test-session membership and manifests passed; 512 windows / 67 recordings |\n| Independent comparison rerun | JSON byte-identical with fixed bootstrap seed; 512 windows / 67 recordings |\n| Training focused suite | 26 passed across comparison, metrics and signal-feature baseline tests |\n| Data ingestion | 60 passed in the component virtual environment, including TimeNet coverage |\n| Inference, `python3 -m unittest discover -s inference -p 'test_*.py' -v` | 28 passed with temporary localhost server permission |\n| Frontend, `npm test` | 81 passed, including structured prediction isolation, model/measurement disagreement, investigation provenance and replay contracts |\n| Frontend, `npm run build` | Passed; existing optional 3D chunk remains over Vite's 500 kB advisory threshold |\n| Ruff, new comparison module/tests | Passed |\n| Clean environment dependency compatibility | `pip check` passed |\n| Real TimeNet round trip | Both dataset parts passed 17 checks each, including all torque/position values and annotation index/time conversion |\n| Private deployed model | Ready; expected canary-v4 SHA-256; real `inference.smoke` passed |\n| Browser, desktop | Structured prediction, J4/J2 review note, predicted 3D moment, seven-channel evidence, reference comparison and Markdown export verified through the live UI |\n| Actual downloaded investigation JSON | Verified 1,024 raw samples/channel, seven measurement rows, one in-window publisher marker, expected checkpoint and matching model receipt |\n\nThe complete optional training suite initially could not collect `test_checkpoints.py` without PyTorch. The focused suite above is the completed local check, not a claim that GPU training or that checkpoint test was rerun. The live smoke confirms actual inference with the existing deployed checkpoint.\n\nThe clean environment also exposed a pre-existing macOS test-path comparison (`/var` versus resolved `/private/var`); the assertion now compares resolved paths. PyArrow printed restricted CPU-cache probes under the sandbox but completed build/load/validation successfully.\n\n## Integration boundary\n\nPR #19 is the only open pull request. It joins approved dataset sourcing to one-shot ingestion and remains outside the frozen two-minute demo until its broad service and UI changes receive a dedicated review. Training artifacts are scored from their saved predictions; training processes and model deployment were not modified. CNN results remain evaluation-only because no CNN checkpoint or inference endpoint is connected.\n\n## Remaining evidence gaps\n\n- No timed engineer study, customer testimonial or measured ROI.\n- No plain-Llama numerical/text baseline; Qwen is the available zero-shot comparator and has an explicit contact-field failure.\n- No newly untouched confirmatory test after the team inspected these results.\n- No full-corpus TimeNet rebuild in this verification; two full recordings were validated.\n- Checkpoint delivery to the challenge is still an external submission step. The backbone file identity is resolved by hash in `CHECKPOINT_HANDOFF.md`, but the live receipt still reports an unknown backbone revision.\n- No multi-recording raw-window demo, live control, verified root cause or physical contact localization.\n# Trace → OpenTSLM on Nebius\n\nStatus (13 September 2026): deployed privately on the team's Nebius H100 VM in\n`/home/samet/trace-inference`, using Python 3.12 and an isolated virtual environment.\nThe KUKA OpenTSLM-SP adapter is loaded and the SSH tunnel, Vite proxy and real\n1,024-sample inference path pass. The service reports the exact checkpoint digest;\nit never silently switches to a newer training artifact. The current process uses\nCPU because a teammate's training run owns the GPU; do not restart it on CUDA until\nthe training owner confirms that GPU memory is available.\n\nA training endpoint does not serve predictions automatically: this separate process\nloads an exported checkpoint once, then handles on-demand requests from Trace.\n\n### Resume this deployment\n\nThe VM is `samet@89.169.110.3`. After your Hugging Face account has access to the\nbase model, authenticate privately (do not paste tokens into chat or source code):\n\n```bash\nssh -t samet@89.169.110.3 '~/trace-inference/inference/.venv/bin/hf auth login'\n```\n\nThe process is in the `trace-inference` tmux session. After authentication, restart\nthat process only; the inference service does not automatically reload failed\nweights. From inside the VM:\n\n```bash\ntmux respawn-pane -k -t trace-inference -c /home/samet/trace-inference 'exec env TRACE_MODEL_CONFIG=inference/kuka-sp-canary-v4.config.json TRACE_DEVICE=cpu OMP_NUM_THREADS=4 MKL_NUM_THREADS=4 inference/.venv/bin/python -m inference.server >> inference/server.log 2>&1'\ncurl http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/health\n```\n\nWait for `ready: true` before running the smoke test. The local frontend now uses\n`VITE_API_BASE_URL=/api` in gitignored `.env.local`; reconnect the SSH tunnel below\nif the local session ends. This VM has no passwordless sudo; its environment was\ncreated with the official user-local `uv` installer without changing system Python:\n\n```bash\n~/.local/bin/uv venv --python /usr/bin/python3 ~/trace-inference/inference/.venv\n~/.local/bin/uv pip install --python ~/trace-inference/inference/.venv/bin/python -r ~/trace-inference/inference/requirements.txt\n```\n\nDo not recreate the existing environment just to restart the service.\n\n## 1. Find the machine and checkpoint\n\nIn the Nebius console, choose your team's project, then **Compute → Virtual\nmachines**. Open a running VM and inspect its GPU type/count, public IP and SSH\nconnection instructions. If the list is empty, check other projects/regions.\nAn allocated training job is not necessarily a persistent VM you can SSH into.\n\nAsk Atakan for:\n\n> Which architecture are you training (OpenTSLM-SP or Flamingo), which base LLM,\n> and where is the exported checkpoint? Please share the training config, including\n> normalization, channel order, sampling/window length, prompt template and LoRA\n> settings. Is there a GPU VM we can use for inference without competing with training?\n\nThe first runtime supports **OpenTSLM-SP** with strict encoder/projector loading\nand explicit LoRA settings. The initial config uses the official\n`OpenTSLM/llama-3.2-1b-tsqa-sp` checkpoint. It was trained on TSQA, **not KUKA**;\nseven robot channels and this prompt are out-of-domain. A plausible answer is\nnot a model-quality result. Keep local numerical calculations as the factual check.\n\nFor Flamingo or a modified training fork, share the config first: upstream uses a\ndifferent state format and permissive checkpoint loading. This service deliberately\nfails on an unsupported architecture instead of silently serving partial weights.\n\n## 2. Prepare one GPU VM\n\nIf no suitable VM exists, create a plain GPU VM in Nebius with an Ubuntu CUDA\nimage and your **public** SSH key. One GPU is the starting point; check the available\nGPU's price, memory and quota before creation. A GPU cluster/Kubernetes is unnecessary\nfor this single-process smoke test. Use persistent storage for model caches and\ncheckpoints, and enough free disk for PyTorch/CUDA dependencies and weights (100 GiB\nis a practical starting allocation, not a measured minimum).\n\nPrefer a separate GPU or run after training pauses; two processes can exhaust VRAM.\nFrom your Mac, test the connection using the username from the VM configuration:\n\n```bash\nssh YOUR_USER@YOUR_VM_IP\nnvidia-smi\npython3 --version\n```\n\nThese commands assume Python 3.12 on the VM. The supplied requirements pin the\nupstream source and core ML dependencies; actual CUDA execution still needs its\nfirst GPU smoke test. If necessary on Ubuntu:\n\n```bash\nsudo apt-get update\nsudo apt-get install -y python3-venv git tmux\n```\n\n## 3. Copy this isolated service\n\nFrom the repository root on your Mac (this also works before committing):\n\n```bash\nssh YOUR_USER@YOUR_VM_IP 'mkdir -p ~/trace-inference/inference ~/trace-inference/frontend/public/data'\nscp inference/*.py inference/requirements.txt inference/smoke.config.json YOUR_USER@YOUR_VM_IP:~/trace-inference/inference/\nscp frontend/public/data/kuka-demo.json YOUR_USER@YOUR_VM_IP:~/trace-inference/frontend/public/data/\n```\n\nOn the VM:\n\n```bash\ncd ~/trace-inference\npython3 -m venv inference/.venv\nsource inference/.venv/bin/activate\npython -m pip install --upgrade pip\npython -m pip install -r inference/requirements.txt\npython -m pip check\npython -m pip freeze > inference/requirements.resolved.txt\npython -c 'import torch; print(torch.__version__); print(torch.cuda.is_available())'\nhf auth login\n```\n\nThe Llama backbone may require your Hugging Face account to have accepted its\nlicense/access terms. Authenticate on the VM interactively; never put its token in\nthe frontend, a URL, a screenshot or a committed config. Use only checkpoints from\nthe official publisher or your team's trusted training process.\n\nStart a persistent terminal session, then the server:\n\n```bash\ntmux new -s trace-inference\ncd ~/trace-inference\nsource inference/.venv/bin/activate\nTRACE_DEVICE=cuda python -m inference.server 2>&1 | tee inference/server.log\n```\n\nTo expose every authorized source recording through the same replay contract, mount\nthe trusted extracted KUKA archive and pass its root explicitly:\n\n```bash\nTRACE_DEVICE=cuda python -m inference.server --raw-root /absolute/path/to/kuka/raw\n```\n\nThe root must contain `<archive>/<recording-id>/JK_MsrExtTrq.mat` and the matching\n`JK_moments.mat`. At startup the adapter validates the original 1 kHz time axis,\nseven-channel shape, finite values, publisher marker bounds and file hashes. The\nfrontend receives a 100 Hz overview, then requests an exact 1,024-sample raw window\nonly for the selected analysis interval. It never resamples raw model input.\n\nDetach with Ctrl+B, then D. The server binds to **127.0.0.1:8000** by default. Keep\nit private; this development bridge is not an authenticated public API. It exposes\nreadiness at `/api/health` and actual availability at `/api/models` while weights\nload. No successful placeholder response is substituted on failure.\n\n## 4. Connect Trace and run the real smoke test\n\nOn your Mac, keep this tunnel running:\n\n```bash\nssh -N -L 127.0.0.1:8000:127.0.0.1:8000 YOUR_USER@YOUR_VM_IP\n```\n\nFrom the repository root, in another terminal:\n\n```bash\ncurl http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/health\npython3 -m inference.smoke\n```\n\nThe smoke command fails unless it gets a completed answer, checkpoint identity,\nthe exact selected evidence window and a receipt for **1,024 samples per channel**.\nIt saves `inference/smoke-result.json` (gitignored). Server logs also record an input\nhash. This checks the application path and interval handling; it is not an independent\nGPU attestation or an accuracy benchmark.\n\nThen restart Vite with connected services enabled:\n\n```bash\ncd frontend\nVITE_API_BASE_URL=/api npm run dev -- --port 5174\n```\n\nThe existing `/api` development proxy targets the local SSH tunnel. Alternatively\nset `VITE_API_BASE_URL=/api` in `frontend/.env.local`, then restart Vite. For a\ndeployed frontend, configure a same-origin reverse proxy; Vite's dev proxy is not\nincluded in a production build.\n\nOpen Trace and keep the initial interval **[5.787, 6.811) seconds**. The header must\nshow **OpenTSLM connected**. Click **Analyze interval** and wait for the structured\nprediction, measurements, input receipt and handoff actions. Only OpenTSLM is exposed\nby this service; there is no live CNN selector or endpoint. The measurements-only\nmode remains available under **Ask a custom question**.\n\nWith `--raw-root`, the backend catalog also lists the validated full recordings.\nOpen one, choose any publisher marker, and run the resulting 1.024-second interval.\nThe UI loads that exact raw interval on demand and preserves its input receipt in\nthe exported investigation. Publisher markers remain annotations and are never sent\nto the model.\n\n## 5. Switch to the team's checkpoint\n\nCopy `smoke.config.json` to a gitignored `inference/team.local.json`. Set:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"architecture\": \"sp\",\n  \"model_id\": \"team-kuka-sp\",\n  \"base_model\": \"meta-llama/Llama-3.2-1B\",\n  \"checkpoint_path\": \"/absolute/persistent/path/to/exported-checkpoint.pt\",\n  \"normalization\": \"zscore_sample\",\n  \"max_new_tokens\": 128\n}\n```\n\nThis example is **not** a guess at your team's training configuration. Confirm every\nfield and align `prepare_sample()` with its prompt and preprocessing before using\nthe trained model. Supported normalization options are `zscore_sample` (per channel,\nsample standard deviation; same numerical convention as upstream TSQA) and `none`.\nIf training used LoRA, add `lora` with the exact `lora_r`, `lora_alpha`,\n`lora_dropout` and `target_modules` passed to upstream `enable_lora()`.\n\n```bash\nTRACE_MODEL_CONFIG=inference/team.local.json TRACE_DEVICE=cuda python -m inference.server\n```\n\nStop the previous server before starting the replacement. Re-run the smoke test,\nthen evaluate held-out recordings against the CNN and numerical baseline.\n\n### Promote an evaluated training run\n\nNew training runs now write tensor-only `best_model.pt` and `last_model.pt` files\nthat the inference runtime can load with `weights_only=True`. After\n`post_training.py` reaches `state: complete`, stage the exact artifact and generate\nthe next service config:\n\n```bash\npython -m inference.release \\\n  --candidate /path/to/run/best_model.pt \\\n  --post-training-status /path/to/run/post_training_status.json \\\n  --active-config inference/kuka-sp-v2.config.json \\\n  --models-dir models \\\n  --output-config inference/kuka-sp-next.config.json\n```\n\nThe command refuses incomplete evaluations, unsafe checkpoint payloads and LoRA\nconfiguration mismatches. It stores an immutable digest-named checkpoint and a\nrelease manifest. Review the held-out metrics, point the tmux startup command at\nthe generated config, restart once, and run `python -m inference.smoke`. Keep the\nprevious config until the smoke test passes so rollback is one restart.\n\n## Boundaries and validation\n\n- Queries retrieve signals server-side by recording/window/channel IDs. Annotation\n  labels never enter model input; each request remains fixed if playback changes.\n- This bundled fixture contains raw 1 kHz data only in **[4, 9) seconds**. Inference\n  rejects other ranges and any window that is not exactly 1.024 seconds. It does not feed the reduced\n  100 Hz overview to the model. Full recording inference needs the ingestion/data\n  team to supply raw windows behind the same API.\n- No resampling: the selected channels are normalized individually and zero-padded\n  to a multiple of four for the upstream encoder. The initial interval needs no\n  padding. Ordering follows the submitted channel IDs.\n- One query at a time. Cancellation requests stop generation at the next decoding\n  step; the slot stays occupied until the model call returns. A GPU operation already\n  running cannot be forcibly interrupted by HTTP cancellation. The 120-second\n  stopping criterion also acts between decoding steps, not as a process watchdog.\n- Evidence links identify **input telemetry**, not verified causal explanations.\n  Checkpoint SHA256, configuration hash and resolved backbone revision accompany\n  model identity; an input hash/sample count is included in the completion receipt.\n- CPU-only tests use an explicitly injected test double and do not establish that\n  the checkpoint runs on CUDA. Run from the repository root:\n\n```bash\npython3 -m unittest discover -s inference -p 'test_*.py' -v\n```\n\nOfficial references: [Nebius VM quickstart](https://docs.nebius.com/compute/quickstart),\n[Nebius SSH connection](https://docs.nebius.com/compute/virtual-machines/connect),\n[OpenTSLM source](https://github.com/OpenTSLM/OpenTSLM/tree/2968f4b891baab4307f7e9d0043e87677b593a30),\n[TSQA checkpoint](https://huggingface.co/OpenTSLM/llama-3.2-1b-tsqa-sp).\n\n## Exact input contract and fixed UI examples\n\nEvery inference request now requires `joint_1` through `joint_7` in that order,\na half-open 1.024-second window, and exactly 1,024 contiguous raw samples at 1 kHz.\nBoth the HTTP bridge and model preparation reject mismatches; no resampling or\npadding is used to repair a selection. Display endpoints still accept arbitrary\nintervals and channel subsets. All model input must precede the replay cursor.\n\n`demo_cases.json` adds three fixed UI examples and records original source hashes,\nfixture hashes and selection policy. The two gzip files in `demo_data/` preserve\noriginal torque values (no decimal rounding), a 100 Hz overview, and `[0,8)` raw\ndetail. Build them with scipy/numpy installed:\n\n```sh\npython -m inference.build_demo_cases --raw-root /path/to/data/raw --splits /path/to/data/prepared/v1/splits.json\n```\n\nThe files are checked in; serving them adds no scientific Python dependency.\nDefault startup loads this catalogue; `--data` retains an isolated single-fixture\nserver for tests. `GET /api/demo-cases` provides fixed navigation selections and\nnotes. `GET /api/recordings/{id}/replay` returns the validated overview, raw detail,\nannotations and recording metadata without losing the raw excerpt. Existing\nrecording signals/events endpoints resolve each recording independently.\n\nVerify each example through the same frontend proxy:\n\n```sh\npython -m inference.smoke --base-url http://127.0.0.1:5174/api --case accidental --output /tmp/collision.json\npython -m inference.smoke --base-url http://127.0.0.1:5174/api --case intentional --output /tmp/contact.json\npython -m inference.smoke --base-url http://127.0.0.1:5174/api --case free --output /tmp/free.json\n```\n\nThese are connection checks, not evaluation. The intentional example's source\nrecording belongs to the test split; collision/free belong to training. None is\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":2208},"formatted_output":"# Two-minute demo\n\n## Preparation\n\nFollow `inference/README.md` to restore the private tunnel; do not restart training or change the served checkpoint. Start the frontend with `VITE_API_BASE_URL=/api`. Run `python3 -m inference.smoke` and check the expected checkpoint digest. Open the initial `[5.787,6.811)` second selection with replay at 8 seconds. This recording is a development example, not the held-out accuracy demonstration.\n\nHave the audited comparison and the exported JSON report open beside the app. If the live service is unavailable, explicitly present the committed smoke result as a previously recorded run; never present it as fresh inference.\n\nTiming, measured 13 September 2026 on the current CPU-only deployment (two smoke runs through the SSH tunnel): one full analyze-interval round trip takes about **24 seconds**. For a live run, trigger **Analyze interval** while narrating the publisher-marker segment so the answer is ready by the analysis beat, or pre-run one analysis before starting the clock and run the second one live. Only one query is served at a time; do not queue clicks.\n\n## Script and actions\n\n| Time | Action | Suggested narration |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0:00–0:15 | Start on the selected publisher event and click **Analyze interval** | “An incident was annotated at 6.187 seconds. A controls engineer receives that timestamp and seven synchronized traces, then has to prepare a handoff for the controls lead or vendor.” |\n| 0:15–0:35 | Replay the 1.024-second interval at 0.5× while inference runs | “Trace keeps the publisher annotation, exact 7×1,024 model input and replay context together. The marker starts the investigation; it is not a verified cause or model detection.” |\n| 0:35–0:55 | Read the prediction and click **Show predicted moment in 3D** | “OpenTSLM predicts accidental contact, J4 and a relative onset. The robot jumps to that generated time cue. This is recorded articulation, not a reconstructed collision.” |\n| 0:55–1:15 | Point to **Cross-check**, then click **Inspect exact seven-channel input** | “The model predicts J4 as strongest, while J2 has the largest raw torque range. Those are different rankings. Trace links both back to the exact signals instead of hiding the distinction in prose.” |\n| 1:15–1:30 | Click **Compare with a reference window** | “Against an earlier raw window, J2's measured range increases by 2.963 newton-metres. We ask the engineer to confirm motion phase and payload before attributing a cause.” |\n| 1:30–1:42 | Click **Export incident handoff** | “The Markdown report carries the recording, interval, annotation, measurements, model revision, input receipt, review note and limitations to the next engineer.” |\n| 1:42–1:55 | Open **Evaluation** | “On 512 windows from 67 held-out recording groups, simple features win fixed classification. OpenTSLM adds the readable evidence-linked investigation object, but only 77.93 percent of generations are usable. We show that weakness.” |\n| 1:55–2:00 | Return to the incident | “Trace turns one recorded stop into a reviewable handoff. The business hypothesis is less report assembly and back-and-forth; the prepared pilot still has to measure it.” |\n\nDo not memorize a generated answer as a guaranteed future result. Read the actual response. The model and measured quantities can disagree; that is a reason to inspect evidence. The exact archived live response is in `demo/smoke-result.json`.\n\n## Jury questions\n\n**Why use a TSLM when the feature baseline wins?** Use the feature baseline when the task is only a fixed label. Trace tests a different benefit: one model output that carries event type, relative timing and joint attribution into a readable, evidence-linked investigation handoff. We have not yet measured whether that handoff benefit justifies the lower reliability. We do not claim the TSLM is necessary for calculating peaks, ranges or the fixed benchmark labels.\n\n**How is this faster than just looking at charts?** The manual workflow is scrolling seven synchronized 1 kHz traces and writing notes by hand; in Trace the same incident is marker → analyze → linked evidence → export in a few interactions, as shown in the demo. We deliberately state this as a workflow demonstration, not a measured time saving: the timed engineer study is prepared (`PILOT.md`) but has not been run.\n\n**What is agentic?** The existing workflow composes deterministic measurement tools with a specialized trained model. The separate dataset-scout PR handles bounded sourcing and review. Bounded documentation search is merged infrastructure, not a claimed live forensic RAG agent.\n\n**What business value is demonstrated?** A traceable investigation and export workflow is demonstrated. Reduced debugging time is a hypothesis with a defined pilot; no customer ROI or downtime reduction has been measured.\n\n**Can it find an unknown event anywhere in a run?** The demo opens publisher-marked intervals. General unannotated incident localization and multi-recording raw-window inference are not demonstrated by the bundled fixture.\n# Verification, 13 September 2026\n\nBase: main `db68e6c`; final hardening is on `codex/final-submission-hardening`. Entire is enabled for the worktree; Codex hooks are installed and approval records are present.\n\n## Checks performed\n\n| Check | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Comparison replay, Python standard library | Checksums, identical IDs/targets, test-session membership and manifests passed; 512 windows / 67 recordings |\n| Independent comparison rerun | JSON byte-identical with fixed bootstrap seed; 512 windows / 67 recordings |\n| Training focused suite | 26 passed across comparison, metrics and signal-feature baseline tests |\n| Data ingestion | 60 passed in the component virtual environment, including TimeNet coverage |\n| Inference, `python3 -m unittest discover -s inference -p 'test_*.py' -v` | 28 passed with temporary localhost server permission |\n| Frontend, `npm test` | 81 passed, including structured prediction isolation, model/measurement disagreement, investigation provenance and replay contracts |\n| Frontend, `npm run build` | Passed; existing optional 3D chunk remains over Vite's 500 kB advisory threshold |\n| Ruff, new comparison module/tests | Passed |\n| Clean environment dependency compatibility | `pip check` passed |\n| Real TimeNet round trip | Both dataset parts passed 17 checks each, including all torque/position values and annotation index/time conversion |\n| Private deployed model | Ready; expected canary-v4 SHA-256; real `inference.smoke` passed |\n| Browser, desktop | Structured prediction, J4/J2 review note, predicted 3D moment, seven-channel evidence, reference comparison and Markdown export verified through the live UI |\n| Actual downloaded investigation JSON | Verified 1,024 raw samples/channel, seven measurement rows, one in-window publisher marker, expected checkpoint and matching model receipt |\n\nThe complete optional training suite initially could not collect `test_checkpoints.py` without PyTorch. The focused suite above is the completed local check, not a claim that GPU training or that checkpoint test was rerun. The live smoke confirms actual inference with the existing deployed checkpoint.\n\nThe clean environment also exposed a pre-existing macOS test-path comparison (`/var` versus resolved `/private/var`); the assertion now compares resolved paths. PyArrow printed restricted CPU-cache probes under the sandbox but completed build/load/validation successfully.\n\n## Integration boundary\n\nPR #19 is the only open pull request. It joins approved dataset sourcing to one-shot ingestion and remains outside the frozen two-minute demo until its broad service and UI changes receive a dedicated review. Training artifacts are scored from their saved predictions; training processes and model deployment were not modified. CNN results remain evaluation-only because no CNN checkpoint or inference endpoint is connected.\n\n## Remaining evidence gaps\n\n- No timed engineer study, customer testimonial or measured ROI.\n- No plain-Llama numerical/text baseline; Qwen is the available zero-shot comparator and has an explicit contact-field failure.\n- No newly untouched confirmatory test after the team inspected these results.\n- No full-corpus TimeNet rebuild in this verification; two full recordings were validated.\n- Checkpoint delivery to the challenge is still an external submission step. The backbone file identity is resolved by hash in `CHECKPOINT_HANDOFF.md`, but the live receipt still reports an unknown backbone revision.\n- No multi-recording raw-window demo, live control, verified root cause or physical contact localization.\n# Trace → OpenTSLM on Nebius\n\nStatus (13 September 2026): deployed privately on the team's Nebius H100 VM in\n`/home/samet/trace-inference`, using Python 3.12 and an isolated virtual environment.\nThe KUKA OpenTSLM-SP adapter is loaded and the SSH tunnel, Vite proxy and real\n1,024-sample inference path pass. The service reports the exact checkpoint digest;\nit never silently switches to a newer training artifact. The current process uses\nCPU because a teammate's training run owns the GPU; do not restart it on CUDA until\nthe training owner confirms that GPU memory is available.\n\nA training endpoint does not serve predictions automatically: this separate process\nloads an exported checkpoint once, then handles on-demand requests from Trace.\n\n### Resume this deployment\n\nThe VM is `samet@89.169.110.3`. After your Hugging Face account has access to the\nbase model, authenticate privately (do not paste tokens into chat or source code):\n\n```bash\nssh -t samet@89.169.110.3 '~/trace-inference/inference/.venv/bin/hf auth login'\n```\n\nThe process is in the `trace-inference` tmux session. After authentication, restart\nthat process only; the inference service does not automatically reload failed\nweights. From inside the VM:\n\n```bash\ntmux respawn-pane -k -t trace-inference -c /home/samet/trace-inference 'exec env TRACE_MODEL_CONFIG=inference/kuka-sp-canary-v4.config.json TRACE_DEVICE=cpu OMP_NUM_THREADS=4 MKL_NUM_THREADS=4 inference/.venv/bin/python -m inference.server >> inference/server.log 2>&1'\ncurl http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/health\n```\n\nWait for `ready: true` before running the smoke test. The local frontend now uses\n`VITE_API_BASE_URL=/api` in gitignored `.env.local`; reconnect the SSH tunnel below\nif the local session ends. This VM has no passwordless sudo; its environment was\ncreated with the official user-local `uv` installer without changing system Python:\n\n```bash\n~/.local/bin/uv venv --python /usr/bin/python3 ~/trace-inference/inference/.venv\n~/.local/bin/uv pip install --python ~/trace-inference/inference/.venv/bin/python -r ~/trace-inference/inference/requirements.txt\n```\n\nDo not recreate the existing environment just to restart the service.\n\n## 1. Find the machine and checkpoint\n\nIn the Nebius console, choose your team's project, then **Compute → Virtual\nmachines**. Open a running VM and inspect its GPU type/count, public IP and SSH\nconnection instructions. If the list is empty, check other projects/regions.\nAn allocated training job is not necessarily a persistent VM you can SSH into.\n\nAsk Atakan for:\n\n> Which architecture are you training (OpenTSLM-SP or Flamingo), which base LLM,\n> and where is the exported checkpoint? Please share the training config, including\n> normalization, channel order, sampling/window length, prompt template and LoRA\n> settings. Is there a GPU VM we can use for inference without competing with training?\n\nThe first runtime supports **OpenTSLM-SP** with strict encoder/projector loading\nand explicit LoRA settings. The initial config uses the official\n`OpenTSLM/llama-3.2-1b-tsqa-sp` checkpoint. It was trained on TSQA, **not KUKA**;\nseven robot channels and this prompt are out-of-domain. A plausible answer is\nnot a model-quality result. Keep local numerical calculations as the factual check.\n\nFor Flamingo or a modified training fork, share the config first: upstream uses a\ndifferent state format and permissive checkpoint loading. This service deliberately\nfails on an unsupported architecture instead of silently serving partial weights.\n\n## 2. Prepare one GPU VM\n\nIf no suitable VM exists, create a plain GPU VM in Nebius with an Ubuntu CUDA\nimage and your **public** SSH key. One GPU is the starting point; check the available\nGPU's price, memory and quota before creation. A GPU cluster/Kubernetes is unnecessary\nfor this single-process smoke test. Use persistent storage for model caches and\ncheckpoints, and enough free disk for PyTorch/CUDA dependencies and weights (100 GiB\nis a practical starting allocation, not a measured minimum).\n\nPrefer a separate GPU or run after training pauses; two processes can exhaust VRAM.\nFrom your Mac, test the connection using the username from the VM configuration:\n\n```bash\nssh YOUR_USER@YOUR_VM_IP\nnvidia-smi\npython3 --version\n```\n\nThese commands assume Python 3.12 on the VM. The supplied requirements pin the\nupstream source and core ML dependencies; actual CUDA execution still needs its\nfirst GPU smoke test. If necessary on Ubuntu:\n\n```bash\nsudo apt-get update\nsudo apt-get install -y python3-venv git tmux\n```\n\n## 3. Copy this isolated service\n\nFrom the repository root on your Mac (this also works before committing):\n\n```bash\nssh YOUR_USER@YOUR_VM_IP 'mkdir -p ~/trace-inference/inference ~/trace-inference/frontend/public/data'\nscp inference/*.py inference/requirements.txt inference/smoke.config.json YOUR_USER@YOUR_VM_IP:~/trace-inference/inference/\nscp frontend/public/data/kuka-demo.json YOUR_USER@YOUR_VM_IP:~/trace-inference/frontend/public/data/\n```\n\nOn the VM:\n\n```bash\ncd ~/trace-inference\npython3 -m venv inference/.venv\nsource inference/.venv/bin/activate\npython -m pip install --upgrade pip\npython -m pip install -r inference/requirements.txt\npython -m pip check\npython -m pip freeze > inference/requirements.resolved.txt\npython -c 'import torch; print(torch.__version__); print(torch.cuda.is_available())'\nhf auth login\n```\n\nThe Llama backbone may require your Hugging Face account to have accepted its\nlicense/access terms. Authenticate on the VM interactively; never put its token in\nthe frontend, a URL, a screenshot or a committed config. Use only checkpoints from\nthe official publisher or your team's trusted training process.\n\nStart a persistent terminal session, then the server:\n\n```bash\ntmux new -s trace-inference\ncd ~/trace-inference\nsource inference/.venv/bin/activate\nTRACE_DEVICE=cuda python -m inference.server 2>&1 | tee inference/server.log\n```\n\nTo expose every authorized source recording through the same replay contract, mount\nthe trusted extracted KUKA archive and pass its root explicitly:\n\n```bash\nTRACE_DEVICE=cuda python -m inference.server --raw-root /absolute/path/to/kuka/raw\n```\n\nThe root must contain `<archive>/<recording-id>/JK_MsrExtTrq.mat` and the matching\n`JK_moments.mat`. At startup the adapter validates the original 1 kHz time axis,\nseven-channel shape, finite values, publisher marker bounds and file hashes. The\nfrontend receives a 100 Hz overview, then requests an exact 1,024-sample raw window\nonly for the selected analysis interval. It never resamples raw model input.\n\nDetach with Ctrl+B, then D. The server binds to **127.0.0.1:8000** by default. Keep\nit private; this development bridge is not an authenticated public API. It exposes\nreadiness at `/api/health` and actual availability at `/api/models` while weights\nload. No successful placeholder response is substituted on failure.\n\n## 4. Connect Trace and run the real smoke test\n\nOn your Mac, keep this tunnel running:\n\n```bash\nssh -N -L 127.0.0.1:8000:127.0.0.1:8000 YOUR_USER@YOUR_VM_IP\n```\n\nFrom the repository root, in another terminal:\n\n```bash\ncurl http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/health\npython3 -m inference.smoke\n```\n\nThe smoke command fails unless it gets a completed answer, checkpoint identity,\nthe exact selected evidence window and a receipt for **1,024 samples per channel**.\nIt saves `inference/smoke-result.json` (gitignored). Server logs also record an input\nhash. This checks the application path and interval handling; it is not an independent\nGPU attestation or an accuracy benchmark.\n\nThen restart Vite with connected services enabled:\n\n```bash\ncd frontend\nVITE_API_BASE_URL=/api npm run dev -- --port 5174\n```\n\nThe existing `/api` development proxy targets the local SSH tunnel. Alternatively\nset `VITE_API_BASE_URL=/api` in `frontend/.env.local`, then restart Vite. For a\ndeployed frontend, configure a same-origin reverse proxy; Vite's dev proxy is not\nincluded in a production build.\n\nOpen Trace and keep the initial interval **[5.787, 6.811) seconds**. The header must\nshow **OpenTSLM connected**. Click **Analyze interval** and wait for the structured\nprediction, measurements, input receipt and handoff actions. Only OpenTSLM is exposed\nby this service; there is no live CNN selector or endpoint. The measurements-only\nmode remains available under **Ask a custom question**.\n\nWith `--raw-root`, the backend catalog also lists the validated full recordings.\nOpen one, choose any publisher marker, and run the resulting 1.024-second interval.\nThe UI loads that exact raw interval on demand and preserves its input receipt in\nthe exported investigation. Publisher markers remain annotations and are never sent\nto the model.\n\n## 5. Switch to the team's checkpoint\n\nCopy `smoke.config.json` to a gitignored `inference/team.local.json`. Set:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"architecture\": \"sp\",\n  \"model_id\": \"team-kuka-sp\",\n  \"base_model\": \"meta-llama/Llama-3.2-1B\",\n  \"checkpoint_path\": \"/absolute/persistent/path/to/exported-checkpoint.pt\",\n  \"normalization\": \"zscore_sample\",\n  \"max_new_tokens\": 128\n}\n```\n\nThis example is **not** a guess at your team's training configuration. Confirm every\nfield and align `prepare_sample()` with its prompt and preprocessing before using\nthe trained model. Supported normalization options are `zscore_sample` (per channel,\nsample standard deviation; same numerical convention as upstream TSQA) and `none`.\nIf training used LoRA, add `lora` with the exact `lora_r`, `lora_alpha`,\n`lora_dropout` and `target_modules` passed to upstream `enable_lora()`.\n\n```bash\nTRACE_MODEL_CONFIG=inference/team.local.json TRACE_DEVICE=cuda python -m inference.server\n```\n\nStop the previous server before starting the replacement. Re-run the smoke test,\nthen evaluate held-out recordings against the CNN and numerical baseline.\n\n### Promote an evaluated training run\n\nNew training runs now write tensor-only `best_model.pt` and `last_model.pt` files\nthat the inference runtime can load with `weights_only=True`. After\n`post_training.py` reaches `state: complete`, stage the exact artifact and generate\nthe next service config:\n\n```bash\npython -m inference.release \\\n  --candidate /path/to/run/best_model.pt \\\n  --post-training-status /path/to/run/post_training_status.json \\\n  --active-config inference/kuka-sp-v2.config.json \\\n  --models-dir models \\\n  --output-config inference/kuka-sp-next.config.json\n```\n\nThe command refuses incomplete evaluations, unsafe checkpoint payloads and LoRA\nconfiguration mismatches. It stores an immutable digest-named checkpoint and a\nrelease manifest. Review the held-out metrics, point the tmux startup command at\nthe generated config, restart once, and run `python -m inference.smoke`. Keep the\nprevious config until the smoke test passes so rollback is one restart.\n\n## Boundaries and validation\n\n- Queries retrieve signals server-side by recording/window/channel IDs. Annotation\n  labels never enter model input; each request remains fixed if playback changes.\n- This bundled fixture contains raw 1 kHz data only in **[4, 9) seconds**. Inference\n  rejects other ranges and any window that is not exactly 1.024 seconds. It does not feed the reduced\n  100 Hz overview to the model. Full recording inference needs the ingestion/data\n  team to supply raw windows behind the same API.\n- No resampling: the selected channels are normalized individually and zero-padded\n  to a multiple of four for the upstream encoder. The initial interval needs no\n  padding. Ordering follows the submitted channel IDs.\n- One query at a time. Cancellation requests stop generation at the next decoding\n  step; the slot stays occupied until the model call returns. A GPU operation already\n  running cannot be forcibly interrupted by HTTP cancellation. The 120-second\n  stopping criterion also acts between decoding steps, not as a process watchdog.\n- Evidence links identify **input telemetry**, not verified causal explanations.\n  Checkpoint SHA256, configuration hash and resolved backbone revision accompany\n  model identity; an input hash/sample count is included in the completion receipt.\n- CPU-only tests use an explicitly injected test double and do not establish that\n  the checkpoint runs on CUDA. Run from the repository root:\n\n```bash\npython3 -m unittest discover -s inference -p 'test_*.py' -v\n```\n\nOfficial references: [Nebius VM quickstart](https://docs.nebius.com/compute/quickstart),\n[Nebius SSH connection](https://docs.nebius.com/compute/virtual-machines/connect),\n[OpenTSLM source](https://github.com/OpenTSLM/OpenTSLM/tree/2968f4b891baab4307f7e9d0043e87677b593a30),\n[TSQA checkpoint](https://huggingface.co/OpenTSLM/llama-3.2-1b-tsqa-sp).\n\n## Exact input contract and fixed UI examples\n\nEvery inference request now requires `joint_1` through `joint_7` in that order,\na half-open 1.024-second window, and exactly 1,024 contiguous raw samples at 1 kHz.\nBoth the HTTP bridge and model preparation reject mismatches; no resampling or\npadding is used to repair a selection. Display endpoints still accept arbitrary\nintervals and channel subsets. All model input must precede the replay cursor.\n\n`demo_cases.json` adds three fixed UI examples and records original source hashes,\nfixture hashes and selection policy. The two gzip files in `demo_data/` preserve\noriginal torque values (no decimal rounding), a 100 Hz overview, and `[0,8)` raw\ndetail. Build them with scipy/numpy installed:\n\n```sh\npython -m inference.build_demo_cases --raw-root /path/to/data/raw --splits /path/to/data/prepared/v1/splits.json\n```\n\nThe files are checked in; serving them adds no scientific Python dependency.\nDefault startup loads this catalogue; `--data` retains an isolated single-fixture\nserver for tests. `GET /api/demo-cases` provides fixed navigation selections and\nnotes. `GET /api/recordings/{id}/replay` returns the validated overview, raw detail,\nannotations and recording metadata without losing the raw excerpt. Existing\nrecording signals/events endpoints resolve each recording independently.\n\nVerify each example through the same frontend proxy:\n\n```sh\npython -m inference.smoke --base-url http://127.0.0.1:5174/api --case accidental --output /tmp/collision.json\npython -m inference.smoke --base-url http://127.0.0.1:5174/api --case intentional --output /tmp/contact.json\npython -m inference.smoke --base-url http://127.0.0.1:5174/api --case free --output /tmp/free.json\n```\n\nThese are connection checks, not evaluation. The intentional example's source\nrecording belongs to the test split; collision/free belong to training. None is\n"},"started_at_ms":1789260406490,"completed_at_ms":1789260406490}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-13T00:46:46.540Z","ordinal":5453,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a0983b-1f0c-7763-9ea7-e221b806d32d","call_id":"call_U9mFZWq7NzEXbWPml6oVNUng","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"f97b24\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000002208,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":5812,\"output\":\"# Two-minute demo\\n\\n## Preparation\\n\\nFollow `inference/README.md` to restore the private tunnel; do not restart training or change the served checkpoint. Start the frontend with `VITE_API_BASE_URL=/api`. Run `python3 -m inference.smoke` and check the expected checkpoint digest. Open the initial `[5.787,6.811)` second selection with replay at 8 seconds. This recording is a development example, not the held-out accuracy demonstration.\\n\\nHave the audited comparison and the exported JSON report open beside the app. If the live service is unavailable, explicitly present the committed smoke result as a previously recorded run; never present it as fresh inference.\\n\\nTiming, measured 13 September 2026 on the current CPU-only deployment (two smoke runs through the SSH tunnel): one full analyze-interval round trip takes about **24 seconds**. For a live run, trigger **Analyze interval** while narrating the publisher-marker segment so the answer is ready by the analysis beat, or pre-run one analysis before starting the clock and run the second one live. Only one query is served at a time; do not queue clicks.\\n\\n## Script and actions\\n\\n| Time | Action | Suggested narration |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| 0:00–0:15 | Start on the selected publisher event and click **Analyze interval** | “An incident was annotated at 6.187 seconds. A controls engineer receives that timestamp and seven synchronized traces, then has to prepare a handoff for the controls lead or vendor.” |\\n| 0:15–0:35 | Replay the 1.024-second interval at 0.5× while inference runs | “Trace keeps the publisher annotation, exact 7×1,024 model input and replay context together. The marker starts the investigation; it is not a verified cause or model detection.” |\\n| 0:35–0:55 | Read the prediction and click **Show predicted moment in 3D** | “OpenTSLM predicts accidental contact, J4 and a relative onset. The robot jumps to that generated time cue. This is recorded articulation, not a reconstructed collision.” |\\n| 0:55–1:15 | Point to **Cross-check**, then click **Inspect exact seven-channel input** | “The model predicts J4 as strongest, while J2 has the largest raw torque range. Those are different rankings. Trace links both back to the exact signals instead of hiding the distinction in prose.” |\\n| 1:15–1:30 | Click **Compare with a reference window** | “Against an earlier raw window, J2's measured range increases by 2.963 newton-metres. We ask the engineer to confirm motion phase and payload before attributing a cause.” |\\n| 1:30–1:42 | Click **Export incident handoff** | “The Markdown report carries the recording, interval, annotation, measurements, model revision, input receipt, review note and limitations to the next engineer.” |\\n| 1:42–1:55 | Open **Evaluation** | “On 512 windows from 67 held-out recording groups, simple features win fixed classification. OpenTSLM adds the readable evidence-linked investigation object, but only 77.93 percent of generations are usable. We show that weakness.” |\\n| 1:55–2:00 | Return to the incident | “Trace turns one recorded stop into a reviewable handoff. The business hypothesis is less report assembly and back-and-forth; the prepared pilot still has to measure it.” |\\n\\nDo not memorize a generated answer as a guaranteed future result. Read the actual response. The model and measured quantities can disagree; that is a reason to inspect evidence. The exact archived live response is in `demo/smoke-result.json`.\\n\\n## Jury questions\\n\\n**Why use a TSLM when the feature baseline wins?** Use the feature baseline when the task is only a fixed label. Trace tests a different benefit: one model output that carries event type, relative timing and joint attribution into a readable, evidence-linked investigation handoff. We have not yet measured whether that handoff benefit justifies the lower reliability. We do not claim the TSLM is necessary for calculating peaks, ranges or the fixed benchmark labels.\\n\\n**How is this faster than just looking at charts?** The manual workflow is scrolling seven synchronized 1 kHz traces and writing notes by hand; in Trace the same incident is marker → analyze → linked evidence → export in a few interactions, as shown in the demo. We deliberately state this as a workflow demonstration, not a measured time saving: the timed engineer study is prepared (`PILOT.md`) but has not been run.\\n\\n**What is agentic?** The existing workflow composes deterministic measurement tools with a specialized trained model. The separate dataset-scout PR handles bounded sourcing and review. Bounded documentation search is merged infrastructure, not a claimed live forensic RAG agent.\\n\\n**What business value is demonstrated?** A traceable investigation and export workflow is demonstrated. Reduced debugging time is a hypothesis with a defined pilot; no customer ROI or downtime reduction has been measured.\\n\\n**Can it find an unknown event anywhere in a run?** The demo opens publisher-marked intervals. General unannotated incident localization and multi-recording raw-window inference are not demonstrated by the bundled fixture.\\n# Verification, 13 September 2026\\n\\nBase: main `db68e6c`; final hardening is on `codex/final-submission-hardening`. Entire is enabled for the worktree; Codex hooks are installed and approval records are present.\\n\\n## Checks performed\\n\\n| Check | Result |\\n|---|---|\\n| Comparison replay, Python standard library | Checksums, identical IDs/targets, test-session membership and manifests passed; 512 windows / 67 recordings |\\n| Independent comparison rerun | JSON byte-identical with fixed bootstrap seed; 512 windows / 67 recordings |\\n| Training focused suite | 26 passed across comparison, metrics and signal-feature baseline tests |\\n| Data ingestion | 60 passed in the component virtual environment, including TimeNet coverage |\\n| Inference, `python3 -m unittest discover -s inference -p 'test_*.py' -v` | 28 passed with temporary localhost server permission |\\n| Frontend, `npm test` | 81 passed, including structured prediction isolation, model/measurement disagreement, investigation provenance and replay contracts |\\n| Frontend, `npm run build` | Passed; existing optional 3D chunk remains over Vite's 500 kB advisory threshold |\\n| Ruff, new comparison module/tests | Passed |\\n| Clean environment dependency compatibility | `pip check` passed |\\n| Real TimeNet round trip | Both dataset parts passed 17 checks each, including all torque/position values and annotation index/time conversion |\\n| Private deployed model | Ready; expected canary-v4 SHA-256; real `inference.smoke` passed |\\n| Browser, desktop | Structured prediction, J4/J2 review note, predicted 3D moment, seven-channel evidence, reference comparison and Markdown export verified through the live UI |\\n| Actual downloaded investigation JSON | Verified 1,024 raw samples/channel, seven measurement rows, one in-window publisher marker, expected checkpoint and matching model receipt |\\n\\nThe complete optional training suite initially could not collect `test_checkpoints.py` without PyTorch. The focused suite above is the completed local check, not a claim that GPU training or that checkpoint test was rerun. The live smoke confirms actual inference with the existing deployed checkpoint.\\n\\nThe clean environment also exposed a pre-existing macOS test-path comparison (`/var` versus resolved `/private/var`); the assertion now compares resolved paths. PyArrow printed restricted CPU-cache probes under the sandbox but completed build/load/validation successfully.\\n\\n## Integration boundary\\n\\nPR #19 is the only open pull request. It joins approved dataset sourcing to one-shot ingestion and remains outside the frozen two-minute demo until its broad service and UI changes receive a dedicated review. Training artifacts are scored from their saved predictions; training processes and model deployment were not modified. CNN results remain evaluation-only because no CNN checkpoint or inference endpoint is connected.\\n\\n## Remaining evidence gaps\\n\\n- No timed engineer study, customer testimonial or measured ROI.\\n- No plain-Llama numerical/text baseline; Qwen is the available zero-shot comparator and has an explicit contact-field failure.\\n- No newly untouched confirmatory test after the team inspected these results.\\n- No full-corpus TimeNet rebuild in this verification; two full recordings were validated.\\n- Checkpoint delivery to the challenge is still an external submission step. The backbone file identity is resolved by hash in `CHECKPOINT_HANDOFF.md`, but the live receipt still reports an unknown backbone revision.\\n- No multi-recording raw-window demo, live control, verified root cause or physical contact localization.\\n# Trace → OpenTSLM on Nebius\\n\\nStatus (13 September 2026): deployed privately on the team's Nebius H100 VM in\\n`/home/samet/trace-inference`, using Python 3.12 and an isolated virtual environment.\\nThe KUKA OpenTSLM-SP adapter is loaded and the SSH tunnel, Vite proxy and real\\n1,024-sample inference path pass. The service reports the exact checkpoint digest;\\nit never silently switches to a newer training artifact. The current process uses\\nCPU because a teammate's training run owns the GPU; do not restart it on CUDA until\\nthe training owner confirms that GPU memory is available.\\n\\nA training endpoint does not serve predictions automatically: this separate process\\nloads an exported checkpoint once, then handles on-demand requests from Trace.\\n\\n### Resume this deployment\\n\\nThe VM is `samet@89.169.110.3`. After your Hugging Face account has access to the\\nbase model, authenticate privately (do not paste tokens into chat or source code):\\n\\n```bash\\nssh -t samet@89.169.110.3 '~/trace-inference/inference/.venv/bin/hf auth login'\\n```\\n\\nThe process is in the `trace-inference` tmux session. After authentication, restart\\nthat process only; the inference service does not automatically reload failed\\nweights. From inside the VM:\\n\\n```bash\\ntmux respawn-pane -k -t trace-inference -c /home/samet/trace-inference 'exec env TRACE_MODEL_CONFIG=inference/kuka-sp-canary-v4.config.json TRACE_DEVICE=cpu OMP_NUM_THREADS=4 MKL_NUM_THREADS=4 inference/.venv/bin/python -m inference.server >> inference/server.log 2>&1'\\ncurl http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/health\\n```\\n\\nWait for `ready: true` before running the smoke test. The local frontend now uses\\n`VITE_API_BASE_URL=/api` in gitignored `.env.local`; reconnect the SSH tunnel below\\nif the local session ends. This VM has no passwordless sudo; its environment was\\ncreated with the official user-local `uv` installer without changing system Python:\\n\\n```bash\\n~/.local/bin/uv venv --python /usr/bin/python3 ~/trace-inference/inference/.venv\\n~/.local/bin/uv pip install --python ~/trace-inference/inference/.venv/bin/python -r ~/trace-inference/inference/requirements.txt\\n```\\n\\nDo not recreate the existing environment just to restart the service.\\n\\n## 1. Find the machine and checkpoint\\n\\nIn the Nebius console, choose your team's project, then **Compute → Virtual\\nmachines**. Open a running VM and inspect its GPU type/count, public IP and SSH\\nconnection instructions. If the list is empty, check other projects/regions.\\nAn allocated training job is not necessarily a persistent VM you can SSH into.\\n\\nAsk Atakan for:\\n\\n> Which architecture are you training (OpenTSLM-SP or Flamingo), which base LLM,\\n> and where is the exported checkpoint? Please share the training config, including\\n> normalization, channel order, sampling/window length, prompt template and LoRA\\n> settings. Is there a GPU VM we can use for inference without competing with training?\\n\\nThe first runtime supports **OpenTSLM-SP** with strict encoder/projector loading\\nand explicit LoRA settings. The initial config uses the official\\n`OpenTSLM/llama-3.2-1b-tsqa-sp` checkpoint. It was trained on TSQA, **not KUKA**;\\nseven robot channels and this prompt are out-of-domain. A plausible answer is\\nnot a model-quality result. Keep local numerical calculations as the factual check.\\n\\nFor Flamingo or a modified training fork, share the config first: upstream uses a\\ndifferent state format and permissive checkpoint loading. This service deliberately\\nfails on an unsupported architecture instead of silently serving partial weights.\\n\\n## 2. Prepare one GPU VM\\n\\nIf no suitable VM exists, create a plain GPU VM in Nebius with an Ubuntu CUDA\\nimage and your **public** SSH key. One GPU is the starting point; check the available\\nGPU's price, memory and quota before creation. A GPU cluster/Kubernetes is unnecessary\\nfor this single-process smoke test. Use persistent storage for model caches and\\ncheckpoints, and enough free disk for PyTorch/CUDA dependencies and weights (100 GiB\\nis a practical starting allocation, not a measured minimum).\\n\\nPrefer a separate GPU or run after training pauses; two processes can exhaust VRAM.\\nFrom your Mac, test the connection using the username from the VM configuration:\\n\\n```bash\\nssh YOUR_USER@YOUR_VM_IP\\nnvidia-smi\\npython3 --version\\n```\\n\\nThese commands assume Python 3.12 on the VM. The supplied requirements pin the\\nupstream source and core ML dependencies; actual CUDA execution still needs its\\nfirst GPU smoke test. If necessary on Ubuntu:\\n\\n```bash\\nsudo apt-get update\\nsudo apt-get install -y python3-venv git tmux\\n```\\n\\n## 3. Copy this isolated service\\n\\nFrom the repository root on your Mac (this also works before committing):\\n\\n```bash\\nssh YOUR_USER@YOUR_VM_IP 'mkdir -p ~/trace-inference/inference ~/trace-inference/frontend/public/data'\\nscp inference/*.py inference/requirements.txt inference/smoke.config.json YOUR_USER@YOUR_VM_IP:~/trace-inference/inference/\\nscp frontend/public/data/kuka-demo.json YOUR_USER@YOUR_VM_IP:~/trace-inference/frontend/public/data/\\n```\\n\\nOn the VM:\\n\\n```bash\\ncd ~/trace-inference\\npython3 -m venv inference/.venv\\nsource inference/.venv/bin/activate\\npython -m pip install --upgrade pip\\npython -m pip install -r inference/requirements.txt\\npython -m pip check\\npython -m pip freeze > inference/requirements.resolved.txt\\npython -c 'import torch; print(torch.__version__); print(torch.cuda.is_available())'\\nhf auth login\\n```\\n\\nThe Llama backbone may require your Hugging Face account to have accepted its\\nlicense/access terms. Authenticate on the VM interactively; never put its token in\\nthe frontend, a URL, a screenshot or a committed config. Use only checkpoints from\\nthe official publisher or your team's trusted training process.\\n\\nStart a persistent terminal session, then the server:\\n\\n```bash\\ntmux new -s trace-inference\\ncd ~/trace-inference\\nsource inference/.venv/bin/activate\\nTRACE_DEVICE=cuda python -m inference.server 2>&1 | tee inference/server.log\\n```\\n\\nTo expose every authorized source recording through the same replay contract, mount\\nthe trusted extracted KUKA archive and pass its root explicitly:\\n\\n```bash\\nTRACE_DEVICE=cuda python -m inference.server --raw-root /absolute/path/to/kuka/raw\\n```\\n\\nThe root must contain `<archive>/<recording-id>/JK_MsrExtTrq.mat` and the matching\\n`JK_moments.mat`. At startup the adapter validates the original 1 kHz time axis,\\nseven-channel shape, finite values, publisher marker bounds and file hashes. The\\nfrontend receives a 100 Hz overview, then requests an exact 1,024-sample raw window\\nonly for the selected analysis interval. It never resamples raw model input.\\n\\nDetach with Ctrl+B, then D. The server binds to **127.0.0.1:8000** by default. Keep\\nit private; this development bridge is not an authenticated public API. It exposes\\nreadiness at `/api/health` and actual availability at `/api/models` while weights\\nload. No successful placeholder response is substituted on failure.\\n\\n## 4. Connect Trace and run the real smoke test\\n\\nOn your Mac, keep this tunnel running:\\n\\n```bash\\nssh -N -L 127.0.0.1:8000:127.0.0.1:8000 YOUR_USER@YOUR_VM_IP\\n```\\n\\nFrom the repository root, in another terminal:\\n\\n```bash\\ncurl http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/health\\npython3 -m inference.smoke\\n```\\n\\nThe smoke command fails unless it gets a completed answer, checkpoint identity,\\nthe exact selected evidence window and a receipt for **1,024 samples per channel**.\\nIt saves `inference/smoke-result.json` (gitignored). Server logs also record an input\\nhash. This checks the application path and interval handling; it is not an independent\\nGPU attestation or an accuracy benchmark.\\n\\nThen restart Vite with connected services enabled:\\n\\n```bash\\ncd frontend\\nVITE_API_BASE_URL=/api npm run dev -- --port 5174\\n```\\n\\nThe existing `/api` development proxy targets the local SSH tunnel. Alternatively\\nset `VITE_API_BASE_URL=/api` in `frontend/.env.local`, then restart Vite. For a\\ndeployed frontend, configure a same-origin reverse proxy; Vite's dev proxy is not\\nincluded in a production build.\\n\\nOpen Trace and keep the initial interval **[5.787, 6.811) seconds**. The header must\\nshow **OpenTSLM connected**. Click **Analyze interval** and wait for the structured\\nprediction, measurements, input receipt and handoff actions. Only OpenTSLM is exposed\\nby this service; there is no live CNN selector or endpoint. The measurements-only\\nmode remains available under **Ask a custom question**.\\n\\nWith `--raw-root`, the backend catalog also lists the validated full recordings.\\nOpen one, choose any publisher marker, and run the resulting 1.024-second interval.\\nThe UI loads that exact raw interval on demand and preserves its input receipt in\\nthe exported investigation. Publisher markers remain annotations and are never sent\\nto the model.\\n\\n## 5. Switch to the team's checkpoint\\n\\nCopy `smoke.config.json` to a gitignored `inference/team.local.json`. Set:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"architecture\\\": \\\"sp\\\",\\n  \\\"model_id\\\": \\\"team-kuka-sp\\\",\\n  \\\"base_model\\\": \\\"meta-llama/Llama-3.2-1B\\\",\\n  \\\"checkpoint_path\\\": \\\"/absolute/persistent/path/to/exported-checkpoint.pt\\\",\\n  \\\"normalization\\\": \\\"zscore_sample\\\",\\n  \\\"max_new_tokens\\\": 128\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThis example is **not** a guess at your team's training configuration. Confirm every\\nfield and align `prepare_sample()` with its prompt and preprocessing before using\\nthe trained model. Supported normalization options are `zscore_sample` (per channel,\\nsample standard deviation; same numerical convention as upstream TSQA) and `none`.\\nIf training used LoRA, add `lora` with the exact `lora_r`, `lora_alpha`,\\n`lora_dropout` and `target_modules` passed to upstream `enable_lora()`.\\n\\n```bash\\nTRACE_MODEL_CONFIG=inference/team.local.json TRACE_DEVICE=cuda python -m inference.server\\n```\\n\\nStop the previous server before starting the replacement. Re-run the smoke test,\\nthen evaluate held-out recordings against the CNN and numerical baseline.\\n\\n### Promote an evaluated training run\\n\\nNew training runs now write tensor-only `best_model.pt` and `last_model.pt` files\\nthat the inference runtime can load with `weights_only=True`. After\\n`post_training.py` reaches `state: complete`, stage the exact artifact and generate\\nthe next service config:\\n\\n```bash\\npython -m inference.release \\\\\\n  --candidate /path/to/run/best_model.pt \\\\\\n  --post-training-status /path/to/run/post_training_status.json \\\\\\n  --active-config inference/kuka-sp-v2.config.json \\\\\\n  --models-dir models \\\\\\n  --output-config inference/kuka-sp-next.config.json\\n```\\n\\nThe command refuses incomplete evaluations, unsafe checkpoint payloads and LoRA\\nconfiguration mismatches. It stores an immutable digest-named checkpoint and a\\nrelease manifest. Review the held-out metrics, point the tmux startup command at\\nthe generated config, restart once, and run `python -m inference.smoke`. Keep the\\nprevious config until the smoke test passes so rollback is one restart.\\n\\n## Boundaries and validation\\n\\n- Queries retrieve signals server-side by recording/window/channel IDs. Annotation\\n  labels never enter model input; each request remains fixed if playback changes.\\n- This bundled fixture contains raw 1 kHz data only in **[4, 9) seconds**. Inference\\n  rejects other ranges and any window that is not exactly 1.024 seconds. It does not feed the reduced\\n  100 Hz overview to the model. Full recording inference needs the ingestion/data\\n  team to supply raw windows behind the same API.\\n- No resampling: the selected channels are normalized individually and zero-padded\\n  to a multiple of four for the upstream encoder. The initial interval needs no\\n  padding. Ordering follows the submitted channel IDs.\\n- One query at a time. Cancellation requests stop generation at the next decoding\\n  step; the slot stays occupied until the model call returns. A GPU operation already\\n  running cannot be forcibly interrupted by HTTP cancellation. The 120-second\\n  stopping criterion also acts between decoding steps, not as a process watchdog.\\n- Evidence links identify **input telemetry**, not verified causal explanations.\\n  Checkpoint SHA256, configuration hash and resolved backbone revision accompany\\n  model identity; an input hash/sample count is included in the completion receipt.\\n- CPU-only tests use an explicitly injected test double and do not establish that\\n  the checkpoint runs on CUDA. Run from the repository root:\\n\\n```bash\\npython3 -m unittest discover -s inference -p 'test_*.py' -v\\n```\\n\\nOfficial references: [Nebius VM quickstart](https://docs.nebius.com/compute/quickstart),\\n[Nebius SSH connection](https://docs.nebius.com/compute/virtual-machines/connect),\\n[OpenTSLM source](https://github.com/OpenTSLM/OpenTSLM/tree/2968f4b891baab4307f7e9d0043e87677b593a30),\\n[TSQA checkpoint](https://huggingface.co/OpenTSLM/llama-3.2-1b-tsqa-sp).\\n\\n## Exact input contract and fixed UI examples\\n\\nEvery inference request now requires `joint_1` through `joint_7` in that order,\\na half-open 1.024-second window, and exactly 1,024 contiguous raw samples at 1 kHz.\\nBoth the HTTP bridge and model preparation reject mismatches; no resampling or\\npadding is used to repair a selection. Display endpoints still accept arbitrary\\nintervals and channel subsets. All model input must precede the replay cursor.\\n\\n`demo_cases.json` adds three fixed UI examples and records original source hashes,\\nfixture hashes and selection policy. The two gzip files in `demo_data/` preserve\\noriginal torque values (no decimal rounding), a 100 Hz overview, and `[0,8)` raw\\ndetail. Build them with scipy/numpy installed:\\n\\n```sh\\npython -m inference.build_demo_cases --raw-root /path/to/data/raw --splits /path/to/data/prepared/v1/splits.json\\n```\\n\\nThe files are checked in; serving them adds no scientific Python dependency.\\nDefault startup loads this catalogue; `--data` retains an isolated single-fixture\\nserver for tests. `GET /api/demo-cases` provides fixed navigation selections and\\nnotes. `GET /api/recordings/{id}/replay` returns the validated overview, raw detail,\\nannotations and recording metadata without losing the raw excerpt. Existing\\nrecording signals/events endpoints resolve each recording independently.\\n\\nVerify each example through the same frontend proxy:\\n\\n```sh\\npython -m inference.smoke --base-url http://127.0.0.1:5174/api --case accidental --output /tmp/collision.json\\npython -m inference.smoke --base-url http://127.0.0.1:5174/api --case intentional --output /tmp/contact.json\\npython -m inference.smoke --base-url http://127.0.0.1:5174/api --case free --output /tmp/free.json\\n```\\n\\nThese are connection checks, not evaluation. The intentional example's source\\nrecording belongs to the test split; collision/free belong to training. None is\\n\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0982b-48a6-7a00-b4fb-0f363c602d5d","create_time":1789260406.540272}}}
